<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
    Resources/Vigil-nomulticast.entitlements — the fallback set.

    Byte-for-byte Resources/Vigil.entitlements minus com.apple.developer.networking.multicast.
    Required by docs/spec-discovery.md §9.5 ("Build-script contract") and named in the file
    manifest at docs/API_CONTRACT.md §5.1.

    WHEN THIS FILE IS USED
    com.apple.developer.networking.multicast is a *managed* entitlement: Apple grants it per
    developer team on request, and using it requires a matching provisioning profile embedded in
    the app. Anyone building Vigil from source without that grant — which is everyone, at first —
    needs this file instead. Scripts/build-app.sh selects it automatically whenever
    PROVISIONING_PROFILE is unset, BEFORE signing.

    ⛔ CORRECTED. This paragraph used to say that codesign would "refuse the shipping entitlements"
    and that the build script catches that failure and re-signs. Both halves were wrong, and the
    error was load-bearing: it put the fallback on a code path that never executes.

    codesign does NOT refuse a managed entitlement under an ad-hoc signature. It signs happily, and
    a deep strict verify then reports "valid on disk" and "satisfies its Designated Requirement".
    (Flag names are spelled out in words here on purpose: XML forbids a double hyphen anywhere inside
    a comment, so writing the flags literally makes this file unparseable. Scripts/lint.py enforces
    that, and caught this very paragraph.) The refusal comes later, from **launchd**, which is what
    actually checks an entitlement against a provisioning profile:

        Error Domain=RBSRequestErrorDomain Code=5 "Launch failed."
        NSUnderlyingError=... NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=163
        NSLocalizedDescription=Launchd job spawn failed

    So the app built, signed and verified — and could not start. The selection is now made up front
    from the presence of a provisioning profile, and the reactive fallback is kept only for a real
    signing identity, where codesign genuinely does refuse.

    WHAT THE APP LOSES, AND WHY THAT IS STILL USABLE
    Discovery degrades to a unicast sweep (docs/spec-discovery.md §9.5). Concretely:

      lost   SADP multicast probe to 239.255.255.250:37020
      lost   WS-Discovery multicast probe to 239.255.255.250:3702
      lost   directed and limited broadcast — Apple's entitlement covers broadcast too, so the
             fallback must NOT be designed around it
      kept   unicast SADP to host:37020 and unicast WS-Discovery to host:3702
      kept   the TCP connect sweep, promoted to start at t=0 with more connections in flight
      kept   Bonjour browsing, which goes through the system mDNS daemon rather than our socket

    The one real loss is off-subnet devices: a factory-fresh camera sitting on 192.168.1.64 while
    the Mac is on 10.0.20.0/24 is only reachable via link-local multicast, and that is exactly the
    unboxing case. VigilUI must say so in the discovery sheet rather than showing an empty list.

    The app does not know at compile time which file was used. It reads its own code signature at
    runtime (docs/spec-discovery.md §9.3) and also verifies empirically, because entitlement
    enforcement differs between macOS 14 and macOS 15. One binary, no compile-time flag.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>

    <key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
    <true/>

    <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- Still required, and still the easiest line to lose: without it the unicast SADP and
         WS-Discovery sockets cannot bind either, and the fallback path finds nothing at all. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.network.server</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- com.apple.developer.networking.multicast is intentionally absent. That is the entire
         difference between this file and Resources/Vigil.entitlements. -->

    <key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- The default clip and snapshot destination, ~/Movies/Vigil.
         Without this, `.moviesDirectory` resolves to `Data/Movies` inside the container, which is a
         symlink to the real folder — so `createDirectory` fails with NSCocoaErrorDomain 513
         (NSFileWriteNoPermissionError) and recording refuses to start. Observed on the first real
         recording attempt; the path in the error is what named the cause.
         `files.user-selected.read-write` below does NOT cover this: it grants only what the user
         picks in a panel, and the default destination is never picked. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.assets.movies.read-write</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- Snapshots land in ~/Pictures/Vigil, the way clips land in ~/Movies/Vigil, and
         they need their own grant: the two asset entitlements are separate keys. Without
         this the sandbox refuses the write with NSCocoaErrorDomain 513 at a path that
         looks perfectly ordinary — which is exactly how the movies grant was found
         missing, after Record appeared to work and produced no file. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.assets.pictures.read-write</key>
    <true/>

    <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
    <true/>

</dict>
</plist>
